.docname {Claims Gateway}
.include {docs}

.glossary
    | Word | Meaning | Example |
    |---|---|---|
    | Keycloak | The sign-in server: it holds passwords and profiles | realm `crowdvision` |
    | ID token | A JWT from Keycloak that says who signed in | `sub`, `preferred_username`, `organization` |
    | JWT | A signed JSON object in three base64 parts: header, claims, signature | `eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1Ni…` |
    | Internal JWT | The token this service signs. Every other service trusts it | `iss: cv-gateway`, 15 min |
    | Claims | What a JWT says about the caller | `sub`, `accountName`, `sid`, `memberships` |
    | RS256 | Signing with an RSA private key; anyone with the public key can check it | Key id `cv-gateway-1` |
    | JWKS | The public keys, published as JSON, that check a signature | `GET /.well-known/jwks.json` |
    | Cookie | A value the browser sends back on every request to the site | `authentication_token` |
    | Edge | The first stop for every browser request: Caddy locally, Istio in the cluster | Caddy `forward_auth` |
    | Claims header | The claims, base64 JSON, that the edge adds for the services behind it | `x-gateway-claims` |
    | HMAC signature | A code from a shared secret and the body that proves a service-to-service call | `X-Signature` |
    | Provision | Make a membership the first time someone signs in | Ada joins `northfield` |

Go service at `backend/claims-gateway`. It swaps a Keycloak sign-in for the platform's own **internal JWT**, and it is the only service that checks a JWT signature. Every other service trusts what the edge passes on. Domain side: [Identity & Tenancy](../architecture/auth-architecture.qd).

- No database. Accounts live in Keycloak, memberships in [`tenancy`](tenancy.qd).
- Edge route `/gateway/*`, **not** gated: a browser has no token before it signs in.

---

## Package layout

| Path | Holds |
|---|---|
| `cmd/gateway/main.go` | Config, OIDC discovery (stops the process if Keycloak is down), wiring, routes, shutdown |
| `internal/config/config.go` | Env vars; loads or generates the RSA key; TTL 15 min |
| `internal/api/handler.go` | `Mount`, every handler, cookie writing, `writeAuthError` |
| `internal/service/exchange.go` | `Exchange`, `RefreshSession`, first-login provisioning, `HighestRole` |
| `internal/service/register.go` | `Login`, `Register` |
| `internal/service/profile.go` | `Profile`, `UpdateProfile`, `ChangePassword` |
| `internal/oidcverifier/verifier.go` | Checks a Keycloak ID token. The only package that speaks OIDC |
| `internal/keycloakadmin/client.go` | Keycloak token and admin endpoints: password grant, create, read, update user, reset password |
| `internal/signer/signer.go` | `Sign` the internal JWT, `JWKS` |
| `internal/tenancyclient/client.go` | Signed calls to `tenancy`: `MembershipsFor`, `Provision` |

`service` holds only interfaces (`Verifier`, `TenancyClient`, `Signer`, `PasswordAuthenticator`, …). Tests pass fakes; `main.go` passes the real clients.

---

## The internal JWT

```json
{"sub":"a1b2c3d4-…","accountName":"Ada Lovelace","sid":"5e0f…","memberships":[{"domain":"northfield","role":"business_admin"}],
 "iss":"cv-gateway","iat":1793613600,"exp":1793614500}
```

| Claim | From | Rule |
|---|---|---|
| `sub` | Keycloak user id | Required by every reader |
| `accountName` | Keycloak `name`, else `preferred_username` | Display only |
| `sid` | New UUID at sign-in | Kept on refresh and profile edit |
| `memberships` | `tenancy` | Never `null`: no membership is `[]` |
| `iss` | `GATEWAY_ISSUER` | Default `cv-gateway` |
| `iat`, `exp` | Clock | `exp` = `iat` + 15 min (900 s) |

Header: `alg RS256`, `kid` = `GATEWAY_KEY_ID` (default `cv-gateway-1`).

It travels as:

- the `authentication_token` cookie: `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax`, path `/`, no `Max-Age` (gone when the browser closes);
- and, from sign-in routes only, the JSON body `{"token":"…"}`.

---

## Routes

| Method, path | Who may call | Does | Answers |
|---|---|---|---|
| `GET /health` | anyone | Liveness | `200` |
| `GET /.well-known/jwks.json` | anyone | Public key | `200` |
| `POST /exchange` | anyone | Keycloak ID token in, internal JWT out | `200`, `400`, `401`, `503` |
| `POST /login` | anyone | Email or username and password | `200`, `400`, `401`, `503` |
| `POST /register` | anyone | New Keycloak user, then login | `200`, `400`, `409`, `503` |
| `POST /logout` | anyone | Clears the cookie | `204` |
| `GET /me` | valid internal JWT | The caller's claims | `200`, `401` |
| `POST /refresh` | valid internal JWT | New JWT with current memberships | `204`, `401`, `503` |
| `GET /verify` | valid internal JWT | Claims in the `X-Gateway-Claims` reply header, for Caddy | `200`, `401` |
| `GET /profile` | valid internal JWT | Email, name, picture from Keycloak | `200`, `404`, `503` |
| `PATCH /profile` | valid internal JWT | Change email or name, new cookie | `204`, `400`, `404`, `409`, `503` |
| `POST /profile/password` | valid internal JWT | Check the old password, set the new one | `204`, `400`, `401`, `404`, `503` |

"Valid internal JWT" means `RequireAuthentication` from [Auth Middleware](../packages/auth-middleware.qd):

1. Take `Authorization: Bearer …`, else the `authentication_token` cookie. None: `401 unauthenticated`.
2. Parse with this service's own JWKS. Only `RS256`; `iss` must equal `GATEWAY_ISSUER`; `exp` must be in the future. Fails: `401 invalid token`.
3. `sub` empty: `401 invalid token claims`.
4. Put the claims on the request context.

### Error to status

`writeAuthError`:

| Service error | Status |
|---|---|
| `ErrInvalidIDToken` | `401 invalid id token` |
| `ErrInvalidCredentials` | `401 invalid credentials` |
| `ErrEmailTaken` | `409 email already registered` |
| `ErrUserNotFound` | `404 account not found` |
| `ErrTenancyUnavailable`, `ErrKeycloakUnavailable` | `503 service unavailable, try again` |
| anything else | `500 internal error` |

---

## Sign-in end to end

The browser signs in at Keycloak (PKCE), gets an ID token, then calls `/gateway/exchange` (`frontend/src/stores/authentication/authentication.ts`).

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant H as exchangeHandler
        participant X as Gateway.Exchange
        participant V as oidcverifier.Verify
        participant T as tenancyclient
        participant Tn as tenancy
        participant S as signer.Sign
        B->>H: POST /gateway/exchange {"idToken":"eyJ…"}
        H->>H: idToken present?
        H->>X: Exchange(ctx, idToken)
        X->>V: Verify(idToken)
        V->>V: signature from Keycloak JWKS, iss, aud, exp
        V-->>X: IDTokenClaims{Sub, Name, Organization, Roles}
        X->>T: MembershipsFor(sub)
        T->>Tn: GET /internal/memberships?accountId=sub<br/>X-Signature
        Tn-->>T: 200 [memberships]
        alt no memberships and token names an organization
            X->>X: provisionOnFirstLogin(kc)
        end
        X->>S: Sign(StandardClaims{sub, accountName, new sid, memberships}, 15 min)
        S-->>X: JWT
        X-->>H: JWT
        H-->>B: 200 {"token":"eyJ…"}<br/>Set-Cookie authentication_token

### What the data looks like at each step

Example: Ada, already a `business_admin` of `northfield`.

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"idToken":"eyJhbGciOiJSUzI1NiIs…"}`. Missing or empty: `400 idToken is required` |
| 5 | Keycloak's signature checked with keys from its JWKS. `iss` must equal `OIDC_ISSUER`, `aud` must hold `OIDC_CLIENT_ID` |
| 6 | `IDTokenClaims{Sub:"a1b2…", PreferredUsername:"ada@northfield.org", Name:"Ada Lovelace", Organization:"", Roles:[]}` |
| 8 | `X-Signature` over an empty body |
| 9 | `[{"domain":"northfield","role":"business_admin"}]` |
| 10 | Skipped: Ada has a membership |
| 11 | `sub a1b2…`, `accountName "Ada Lovelace"`, `sid` a new UUID, `exp` = now + 900 s |
| 14 | Same JWT in the body and the cookie |

### The decisions in `Exchange`

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A["ID token"] --> V{"Verify ok?"}
        V -- no --> E1["401 invalid id token"]
        V -- yes --> M{"MembershipsFor ok?"}
        M -- no --> E2["503 tenancy unavailable"]
        M -- yes --> Z{"no memberships<br/>and organization claim set?"}
        Z -- no --> S["Sign"]
        Z -- yes --> P{"Provision answer"}
        P -- "204" --> S1["memberships = organization, highest role"]
        P -- "403 invite-only" --> S2["memberships = empty list"]
        P -- other --> E3["503 tenancy unavailable"]
        S1 --> S
        S2 --> S

### First sign-in through an organization

`provisionOnFirstLogin`:

1. `HighestRole(kc.Roles)`: start at `standard_customer`; keep the ladder role with the biggest weight; ignore roles not on the ladder.
2. `POST /internal/provision` with `{"accountId":sub,"domainName":organization,"role":role,"externalId":sub}`, signed over those bytes.
3. `204`: the token gets that one membership.
4. `403`: the domain is invite-only. The token gets **no** membership and sign-in still succeeds.
5. Anything else, `404` for a missing domain included: sign-in fails with `503`.

`organization` in the ID token is a list; only the first entry is used.

**Example.** Keycloak says `organization: ["northfield"]`, `roles: ["business_staff","offline_access"]`. `HighestRole` gives `business_staff` (`offline_access` is not on the ladder). `northfield` is `open-via-idp`, so `tenancy` answers `204` and the token holds `{northfield, business_staff}`.

---

## Password sign-in and sign-up

Used by the app's own forms (`frontend/src/composables/authentication/useKeycloakAuth.ts`). Both end in `Exchange`, so every way in gives the same token.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant G as Gateway
        participant K as keycloakadmin
        participant KC as Keycloak
        B->>G: POST /gateway/register {email, password, name}
        G->>K: CreateUser(email, password, name)
        K->>KC: client_credentials grant
        KC-->>K: admin access token
        K->>KC: POST /admin/realms/{realm}/users
        KC-->>K: 201, or 409 when the email exists
        G->>G: Login(email, password)
        G->>K: PasswordGrant(email, password)
        K->>KC: password grant, scope openid
        KC-->>K: 200 {id_token}
        G->>G: Exchange(id_token)
        G-->>B: 200 {"token"} + cookie

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 1 | `{"email":"carla@x.org","password":"…","name":"Carla"}`. Missing email or password: `400` |
| 5 | `{"username":"carla@x.org","email":"carla@x.org","enabled":true,"emailVerified":false,"firstName":"Carla","credentials":[{"type":"password","value":"…","temporary":false}]}` |
| 6 | `409`: `409 email already registered` to the browser |
| 9 | Form: `grant_type=password`, `client_id`, `client_secret`, `username`, `password`, `scope=openid`. Keycloak `400` or `401`: `401 invalid credentials` |

`/login` is steps 7 to 12 alone. Every Keycloak call has a 5 s timeout. An admin token is fetched again for each admin call; nothing is cached.

---

## Every later request

The browser sends the cookie; the edge checks it before the request reaches a service.

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant C as Caddy
        participant V as verifyHandler
        participant S as e.g. tenancy
        B->>C: GET /tenancy/me/memberships<br/>Cookie authentication_token
        C->>V: forward_auth: GET /verify, same cookie
        V->>V: RequireAuthentication: RS256, iss, exp
        V->>V: json.Marshal(claims), base64
        V-->>C: 200, X-Gateway-Claims
        C->>S: original request + x-gateway-claims
        S->>S: RequireMeshClaims decodes it

| Step | Data |
|---|---|
| 3 | Expired JWT: `401`, Caddy returns it to the browser, `tenancy` never sees the request |
| 4 | `{"sub":"a1b2…","accountName":"Ada Lovelace","sid":"5e0f…","memberships":[…]}` as base64 |
| 6 | Caddy copies only that one header (`copy_headers X-Gateway-Claims`) |

In the cluster, Istio does steps 2 to 5 itself: `k8s/istio-request-authentication.yml` checks the same JWT with this service's JWKS (fetched through `/gateway/.well-known/jwks.json`) and writes the payload to `x-gateway-claims`. `/verify` is then unused.

---

## Staying signed in

.mermaid
    sequenceDiagram
        autonumber
        participant B as Browser
        participant R as refreshHandler
        participant X as Gateway.RefreshSession
        participant Tn as tenancy
        Note over B: useSessionKeepAlive, every 10 min
        B->>R: POST /gateway/refresh, cookie still valid
        R->>R: RequireAuthentication
        R->>X: RefreshSession(claims)
        X->>Tn: GET /internal/memberships
        Tn-->>X: current memberships
        X->>X: Sign(same sub, accountName, sid + new memberships, 15 min)
        R-->>B: 204, new cookie

- Refresh needs a token that is still valid. An expired one is `401`, and the person signs in again.
- Same `sid`: one sign-in stays one session.
- Also called right after a membership change, so the new role shows at once.

**Example.** Ada signs in at 10:00 (`exp` 10:15). Refresh at 10:10 gives `exp` 10:25, at 10:20 gives 10:35. Her laptop sleeps from 10:21 to 10:40: the cookie expired at 10:35, refresh answers `401`.

---

## Profile and password

| Call | Steps |
|---|---|
| `GET /profile` | Admin token, `GET /admin/realms/{realm}/users/{sub}`, return `{email, name, picture}` |
| `PATCH /profile` | Update email and/or `firstName` in Keycloak. A new name replaces `accountName`; sign again with the same `sid`; new cookie |
| `POST /profile/password` | Read the user's email; password grant with the **current** password (wrong: `401`); then reset to the new one through the admin API |

The current password is checked again on purpose: a stolen session alone cannot change it.

---

## Signing keys

`config.loadOrGenerateKey`:

.mermaid
    flowchart TD
        A{"GATEWAY_PRIVATE_KEY set?"} -- yes --> P["parse PEM, PKCS#8, must be RSA"]
        A -- no --> F{"GATEWAY_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE set?"}
        F -- yes --> R["read file"] --> P
        F -- no --> G["generate 2048-bit key,<br/>kid ephemeral-dev-key, log a warning"]
        P --> K["kid = GATEWAY_KEY_ID or cv-gateway-1"]

A generated key changes at every restart: every cookie signed before it stops working. Fine on a laptop, never in a cluster.

---

## Configuration

| Variable | Default | Use |
|---|---|---|
| `OIDC_ISSUER` | required | `iss` that Keycloak ID tokens carry |
| `OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL` | `OIDC_ISSUER` | How this process reaches Keycloak. The `jwks_uri` from discovery is moved onto this host |
| `OIDC_CLIENT_ID` | required | Client the ID token must be issued to |
| `KEYCLOAK_REALM` | required | Realm; also cut off `OIDC_DISCOVERY_URL` to find Keycloak's base URL |
| `REGISTRATION_CLIENT_ID`, `REGISTRATION_CLIENT_SECRET` | required | Confidential client for password grant and admin calls |
| `TENANCY_URL` | required | Base URL of `tenancy` |
| `INTERNAL_SIGNING_SECRET` | required | HMAC secret shared with `tenancy` |
| `GATEWAY_PRIVATE_KEY`, `GATEWAY_PRIVATE_KEY_FILE` | generated | RSA key, PEM PKCS#8 |
| `GATEWAY_KEY_ID` | `cv-gateway-1` | `kid` |
| `GATEWAY_ISSUER` | `cv-gateway` | `iss` of the internal JWT |
| `ADDR` | `:3000` | Listen address |

Fixed in code: TTL 15 min, 5 s timeout on `tenancy` and Keycloak admin calls.

**Why two Keycloak URLs.** Locally a browser's token says `iss: http://localhost:8090/realms/crowdvision`, while this container reaches Keycloak as `http://keycloak:8080/realms/crowdvision`. The check uses the first; the HTTP calls go to the second.

---

## Tests

```bash
just test claims-gateway
```

| File | Checks |
|---|---|
| `service/exchange_test.go` | Current memberships signed; `name` before `preferred_username`; bad ID token; `tenancy` errors fail closed on sign-in and refresh; first-login provisioning, none without an organization, empty on invite-only, fail on other errors; `HighestRole` |
| `service/register_test.go`, `service/profile_test.go` | Login, sign-up, taken email, profile edit, password checked before reset |
| `api/handler_test.go`, `register_handler_test.go`, `profile_handler_test.go` | Routes, status codes, required fields |
| `api/verify_handler_test.go` | `/verify` writes the header; it decodes through `RequireMeshClaims`; no token is `401` |
| `signer/signer_test.go` | Token checks out as RS256; `exp` from the TTL; JWKS holds the key |
| `oidcverifier/verifier_test.go` | Valid token; `name` and `organization` optional; audience, expiry, key and issuer refused when wrong; `jwks_uri` moved to the discovery host; discovery failures |
| `keycloakadmin/client_test.go` | Each Keycloak call and its error mapping |
| `tenancyclient/client_test.go` | Signed requests; `403` becomes `ErrInviteOnly`; failures are errors, never an empty list |
| `config/*_test.go` | Key from file; missing file fails; realm and secret required; Keycloak base URL |

---

## Gaps

- **A missing domain blocks sign-in.** An ID token naming an organization with no `tenancy` domain gets `404` from provision, and the person cannot sign in at all (`503`).
- **Only the first organization is used.** A person in two Keycloak organizations is provisioned in the first only.
- **A sleeping screen loses the session.** No refresh for 15 min means signing in again.
- **No way to revoke one token.** A stolen JWT works until `exp`; `sid` is not checked anywhere.
- **`EmailVerified` is read and never used.**
